※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4393
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4393 LANG_CONSTINIT_WRITTEN

※ **Not even a constructor writes one.**  The initializers have all run
※ before the first constructor does, so a constructor writing a `constinit`
※ variable would be the second thing to give it a value -- and a reader of the
※ definition would be told one value while the program read another.  What a
※ constructor works out for the rest of the program goes in an ordinary `mut`
※ variable; what is worked out once and then only read is the initializer's.
※
※ Compare: **D**, whose module constructors may initialize an `immutable`
※ variable, which is the one place it is written; **C++**, where a `constinit`
※ variable that is not `const` may be written by anything at any time, and the
※ word says only how it starts; **Go**, whose package `init` functions write
※ variables nothing then stops anyone writing.

let std := ⎕import("std")

constinit let seed: u64 = ⎕auxv(6) ?? 1u64

@[constructor, impure]
fn prepare():
    seed ← 7u64

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    ⎕narrow(seed, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 0u6
